<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Taking Care of Our Class</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> We Are Sweeping the Floors.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Extract specific information from short informational texts. Reading: Demonstrate comprehension of basic descriptions in texts. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can extract specific information from short informational texts about classroom chores (e.g., "We sweep the floor."). Can demonstrate comprehension of basic text descriptions by discussing what each chore means (e.g., "Sweep means clean the floor with a broom."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of compound words and syllable division (e.g., recognizing "whiteboard" as "white-board" with two syllables).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a large picture of a messy classroom and another picture of a clean, organized classroom. Students look at the pictures. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and elicits vocabulary from Lesson 1 like 'chair', 'desk', 'messy', 'clean'. The teacher then asks, "Which classroom do you like?" and "What chores can we do to make our classroom clean?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards with images of classroom chores (e.g., a person sweeping, a person wiping a desk, a person arranging chairs). The teacher says the action and points to the word below the picture: "This is 'sweeping the floor'. We sweep the floor." "This is 'wiping the desk'. We wipe the desk." The teacher models pronunciation of /r/ in 'arrange' and /s/ in 'supply' (e.g., 'cleaning supplies'). The teacher also visually demonstrates the concept of compound words by pointing to "whiteboard" on a flashcard and saying "white-board, two syllables."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> After modeling 'sweep the floor': "Do we use a cloth to sweep the floor?" (No, we use a broom.) After modeling 'wipe the desk': "Is the desk dirty or clean after wiping?" (Clean.)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a short informational text titled "Our Classroom Chores" (or displays it on a projector). The text includes 4-5 sentences describing different classroom cleaning tasks using target vocabulary (e.g., "We are sweeping the floor quickly. Please arrange the chairs neatly. Can you help me wipe the whiteboard and desks?"). The text also includes a simple picture next to each description.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will read and comprehend basic descriptions of classroom chores and extract specific information from short informational texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is this text about?" (Elicit: "Classroom chores" or "Cleaning the classroom").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher reads simple statements, and students indicate true or false based on the text. For example: "The students are arranging the windows." (False) "They are sweeping the floor quickly." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students draw a line from a sentence in the text (e.g., "Wipe the whiteboard.") to a corresponding picture or to the correct cleaning tool (e.g., 'cloth', 'cleaner').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading sentences with present continuous ("We are sweeping."), imperatives ("Sweep the floor."), modal "can" ("Can you help me wipe...?"). Vocabulary: chair, desk, table, whiteboard, sweep, wipe, arrange, tidy, clean, messy, quickly, carefully. Phonemic awareness: Compound words and syllable division (e.g., "whiteboard" as "white-board").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/Cloze reading:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the "Our Classroom Chores" text, but with key vocabulary words missing. Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from a word bank to fill in the blanks. Example: "We are _______ the floor with a broom." (Word bank: organize, sweeping, tidy). The teacher guides students to read the completed sentences aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence-picture matching with decoding:</b> Students receive a new set of sentences related to classroom chores (e.g., "We are cleaning up the trash can.", "Please put away the cleaning supplies."). They read each sentence and match it to the corresponding picture. The teacher highlights words like "whiteboard" and asks students to clap the syllables ("white-board"). The teacher prompts students to notice the /r/ sound in "arrange" and /s/ sound in "supplies" as they read.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Read and identify:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading a short descriptive sentence about a chore (e.g., "Someone is making the floor clean with a broom.") from their worksheet. Their partner listens and points to the action flashcard (e.g., 'sweeping') or says the chore (e.g., "Sweeping the floor!"). The focus is on reading comprehension and accurate identification.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task using language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Classroom Chore Card Reading and Explanation." Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of "Chore Cards." Each card has a short descriptive text of a classroom chore (e.g., "It's time to make the floor tidy. You need a broom and a dustpan. Please sweep the floor carefully.") and an image. Some cards also include a question (e.g., "Can you help me clean this part?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns picking a card and reading the chore description silently first, then aloud to their group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading, the student explains what the chore means or what they need to do, using their own words. For example: "This card says 'sweep the floor.' That means we clean the floor with a broom and a dustpan."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If the card has a question, other group members read and respond, practicing "Can I help you...?" or "Yes, please."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing reading fluency, comprehension, and the use of target vocabulary and grammar, specifically the present continuous, imperatives, and 'can'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment:</b> Teacher observation during "Classroom Chore Card Reading and Explanation" using a simple checklist for each student:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reads chore descriptions with comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Extracts specific information from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes target vocabulary words (e.g., sweep, wipe, arrange, whiteboard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to decode compound words (e.g., whiteboard).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a "Read and Match" worksheet individually. The worksheet contains 3-4 short sentences describing a chore (e.g., "The floor is dirty. We are making it clean with a broom.") and a set of pictures representing these chores. Students read each sentence and draw a line to the correct picture. It also includes identifying simple phonemic awareness elements like circling compound words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in matching sentences to pictures (extracting specific information).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of basic chore descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify compound words (e.g., "whiteboard") from the text.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Today, I can read about ____________ (e.g., "sweeping the floor," "classroom chores").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. One word I learned to read today is ____________ (e.g., "dustpan," "quickly," "whiteboard").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">6-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What part of reading about classroom chores was easy for me today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What was difficult to read, and how can I practice more?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to extract specific information from the short texts and match them to pictures. The compound word activity helped with phonemic awareness. Some students struggled with reading adverbs like "carefully" and "quickly" accurately. I need to provide more explicit modeling and practice for longer vocabulary words. The "Chore Card Reading" task encouraged collaborative reading, and students tried using "please" for politeness effectively. Next lesson will build on this reading comprehension by focusing on active speaking and interaction, using these same vocabulary and grammatical structures in dialogues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on understanding written descriptions of chores. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will use this understanding to create and perform simple dialogues about giving instructions and offering help for classroom chores.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>